    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Sejong, the fourth king of Joseon, reigned from 1418 to 1450 and is widely regarded as the greatest king in Korean history.</li><li>He is remembered above all for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a>, the Korean alphabet published in 1446, and for a reign in which his court produced a native calendar, a rain gauge, vast medical and agricultural texts and new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a>.</li><li>He governed by Confucian principles and restricted Buddhist institutions while privately practising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and North Korean histories stress the feudal burdens on the peasantry under his rule.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1430 Sejong had over 170,000 people polled on a proposed tax reform; the debate that followed lasted 17 years.</p></aside>
    </section>

